Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International
The Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International (CC BY-NC 4.0) license is part of the Creative Commons 4.0 license suite released in 2013. It allows users to share and adapt licensed works for non-commercial purposes, provided proper credit is given and no additional restrictions are applied.

Key facts
Identifier: CC-BY-NC-4.0

Published by: Creative Commons (2013)

Use allowed: Copying, redistribution, remixing, adaptation for non-commercial use

Requirements: Attribution, non-commercial use only, no added legal/technical barriers

URL: creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0

Core permissions and restrictions
The license grants a worldwide, royalty-free, irrevocable right to reproduce, share, and adapt a work, so long as the use is not primarily intended for commercial advantage or monetary compensation. Users must give appropriate credit, link to the license, and indicate modifications. They may not impose technological measures (such as DRM) that limit others from exercising these freedoms. 

Legal scope and rights
CC BY-NC 4.0 covers copyright and similar rights, including sui generis database rights, while explicitly excluding patents and trademarks. Moral rights are waived or not asserted to the extent necessary to enable permitted uses. The license’s terms automatically reinstate rights if violations are corrected within 30 days, a feature introduced with version 4.0. 

Evolution within the 4.0 suite
Version 4.0, finalized on November 25 2013, improved international enforceability, clarified attribution standards, and added compatibility with global copyright frameworks. It removed the requirement to cite a work’s title, standardized the URI attribution rule, and strengthened interoperability across jurisdictions. 

Use and significance
CC BY-NC 4.0 is widely adopted by creators, educators, and cultural institutions seeking to encourage sharing and remixing while restricting commercial exploitation. It is a foundational tool in open-access publishing, academic repositories, and digital media distribution that balances public reuse with protection of commercial interests.